# Supplementary fixed-budget MCQ assessment

`supplementary_budgeted_mcq_nonregression_v1` is a separately adopted endpoint.
The original strict v1/v2 no-truncation requirements remain failed in native
calibration. This supplement cannot replace those results or establish PR10,
Issue23, numerical identity, population quality, or performance qualification.

The protocol reuses all 200 original selected cases, original prompt/token IDs,
gold/parser, ordinary workers, 8192-token budget and v2 collector. Its only new
distinction is between collecting a terminal response and answering correctly.
A `length` response can satisfy collection, but always scores wrong, even if its
text contains the gold letter. Missing responses also score wrong with denominator
200 and additionally fail collection. Invalid answers never receive guessed credit.

Adoption and the native-only snapshot were recorded at
`2026-10-25T23:25:18.535441+00:00`. That partial native snapshot contained 64
returned responses and two length cases. Candidate quality runs were absent from
the workspace inventory; root additionally attested no candidate quality execution.
This is a baseline-calibrated supplement, not an untouched primary endpoint.
The snapshot is deliberately preserved as a partial observation, not final evidence.

The new scorer loads the original strict scorer only after verifying its frozen
SHA. It reuses `score`, `evaluate`, `paired`, `cp`, provenance and mixed-receipt
checks. It does not change those functions or any old result file. The output
contains distinct `original_strict_gate` and `supplementary_gate` objects. Strict
acceptance never changes because of a supplementary pass.

Each required run must return exactly one terminal stop/length response for every
case, with no runtime/API errors, full collection and shutdown. The scorer also
checks exact copied producers, inputs and launch configuration, successful terminal
controller/PID/chronology and exact executed argv/input/output paths, four empty
physical-device process tables, all 25
seed-first admissions, internal/external request bindings, tokenizer bytes, and
the correspondence between returned token IDs and stored text. It requires two
fresh native controls, pinned f7/Core/Ascend sources including existing dirty-state
hashes, the current v2 plan and the calibration's common deterministic/fusion
environment. Recorded full model-weight SHA metadata is bound and current file
sizes/mtimes checked; the scorer does not rehash all model weights or prove which
bytes reached device memory.

The answer gate retains both existing conditions: a nonempty set answered
correctly by both native controls with zero new candidate errors on that set,
and candidate correct count at least the lower native count. The mixed-scope gate
also independently replays hashed scheduler/retirement evidence for all 25
candidate batches. Receipt IDs must bind to admitted same-batch requests at valid
steps; retired D IDs must have been scheduled as D, with distinct pending P IDs.
This is observed transaction progress, not a claim of physical kernel overlap.
Group assessment requires a complete comparable raw reference;
raw/group transitions and raw coverage are reported separately. No new raw-stable
veto is introduced.

Run only after the required control/candidate processes are terminal:

```bash
quality=/root/workspace/muxpd-followup-20261025/quality
/root/workspace/.venv/bin/python "$quality/supplementary/score_supplementary.py" \
  --protocol "$quality/supplementary/protocol.json" --quality-root "$quality" \
  --native1 /absolute/path/to/native1/results/run.json \
  --native2 /absolute/path/to/native2/results/run.json \
  --candidate /absolute/path/to/raw/results/run.json \
  --output /absolute/path/to/new-raw-supplementary-result.json
```

For group, select its run as candidate and add `--raw-reference` with the terminal
raw run. Output paths must be new. Missing/incomplete collection cannot pass;
structurally invalid or mismatched provenance is rejected. All 200 case scores,
truncations, natural-stop invalids, missing/errors, paired transitions and existing
descriptive intervals remain visible. A fixed selected subset is not a random
population sample, and a nonsignificant McNemar test does not prove equivalence.

The original producer/scorer, frozen-v2 directory, earlier attempts and strict
results remain unchanged. New CPU fixtures include gold-letter truncation, missing
response, duplicate/unknown/error/nonterminal output, budget violation, stable-set
veto despite offsetting gain, empty stable set, lower-native floor, missing mixed
coverage, mismatched source/plan, admission/receipt identity and chronology, unrelated launch paths, and terminal
cleanup failures. Thirty CPU fixtures passed; runtime scorer and tests pass Ruff.
The earlier failed intermediate fixture run is retained in cpu-tests-v4.log.
